    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d529">

                <pb facs="0471" n="397" sameAs="frus:frus1865p2/pg397"/>

                <head>Mr. Pike to Mr.
                        Seward</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 175.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">United States Legation,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">The Hague,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1865-09-13"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >September</hi> 13, 1865.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> I am without any of your favors since my
                    last, excepting your No. 248. The annual ceremony of opening the legislative
                    chambers takes place the 18th instant, for which the King and his ministers have
                    already returned to town. The two bodies contrive to have about a dozen sessions
                    a year between them. They practice a very old-fashioned and cumbrous form of
                    legislation, as you may suppose, or they would have no need of such frequent
                    sittings.</p>
                <p>The cattle plague has at length reached Holland, and spread in the interior. It
                    is naturally regarded with alarm in a country so full of domestic animals as
                    this, especially of the bovine race.</p>
                <p>The apprehended visitation of the cholera is now but little feared, as the cool
                    weather already approaches.</p>
                <p>The crops in this part of Europe are generally good, in this country especially
                    so.</p>
                <p>In England there has been much complaint, but the apprehensions of a bad harvest
                    are less now than they were a fortnight ago, the weather since then having been
                    remarkably fine.</p>
                <p>Still, prices have risen, and paying rates for our breadstuffs and provisions
                    seem likely to prevail, a circumstance favorable to our finances.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to be, with great respect, your most obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>JAMES S. PIKE.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward,</hi>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of State.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
